A||N||16||Elenor84||How much do u think a shop will charge for rear main seal replacement?||||11||
Z||000000||Elenor84||04-20-2001||12:21 AM||||Hey guys...I'm leaking a quart a day...it spews out...I'm thinking rear main but praying front...if it is rear...hwo much do u think it will cost?  thx!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||11
Z||000001||1988LRC||04-20-2001||08:23 AM||||a lot, if you have any plans for repairs/rebuilds now would be the time to do them because the labor involved to change engines would be close to the labor time to replace rear main seal||||reg||1
Z||000002||Elenor84||04-20-2001||03:04 PM||||alot as in 500?<BR>alot as in 1000?<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||04-20-2001||07:04 PM||||Wait a minute - you're not sure whether it's the front or the rear seal??  If it's a V6 and it turns out to be the distributor o-ring and you do it yourself, it'll cost maybe a buck... extra, if you do the pickup coil at the same time.<P>Quart a day?  Could it be your oil pressure sending unit leaks?  ||||reg||5
Z||000004||c m west||04-20-2001||07:23 PM||||we would charge $450 for labor and 23.47 in parts.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-20-2001||07:56 PM||||CW however is nice and knows what a Fiero is.... Count on most shops being above that.<P><BR>I'd take it to a car wash and hose off everything you can. By the time you get home you should be able to see where it's comming from.<P>don't rule out the slagging oil pan gasket. See if any pan screws are loose or missing.<P>Put a fresh seal on the oil drian plug. that will leak like mad if it's shot. Those washers are suposed to be changed when the oil is.<P>check the oil filter as well. If that's loose or has a bad seal it will blow oil. so can the oil pressure sender. (My last sender blew over 2 quarts out the thing and was still reading perfect on the gauge.)<P><BR>I just reread that..... sounds ALLOT like a pressure leak. Oil filter, sender, that sort of thing. You'd have to have the rear main out to blow that much oil. The main seals don't have pressurized oil against them. They block splash and what runs allong the crank. (It is posible to blow out a rear main but rare. )<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Elenor84||04-21-2001||03:46 AM||||ok well now I sure hope its not a rear main...i'll go check tomorrow to see were its leaking and I will check all the area's u listed...<P>thx guys...<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierohoho||04-21-2001||03:34 PM||||Check your distributor O-ring and rule that out first, it's the cheapest and they can put out quite a bit of oil. If it is a rear main seal I would check with several transmission shops. They take trannys out for a living and can in my experience do it cheaper than a regular all around shop. I mean you don't go to the doctor to have a tooth drilled do ya! I had my '92 S-10 blazer rear main done for $200.00, granted it's not a fiero but I thought the cost was more than reasonable. But track the leak down first and go from there.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierohoho||04-21-2001||03:40 PM||||Oops! Just checked your web site and see you have a 2.5. My advise was for a 2.8 V6 unless it is a rear main then the tranny shop advice stands. My 2.5's have leaked pretty good around the valve cover gasket and if it has high miles mine burn oil also but not too visibly accept on start up. Good luck.||||reg||11
Z||000009||Elenor84||04-21-2001||07:12 PM||||ok I check out my engine today...its not comin from the distributor...I do need a new gasket for the oil pan and the nut on the oil pan...now I think there is also oil comin from were the trany meets the engine...is that a rear main seal?  If it is then yes its leaking...if thats not the rear main then I will be very happy...thx!<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||04-21-2001||07:15 PM||||Could also be the cam cover. Would require separating engine/tranny same as rear main. If you have that done, might as well replace clutch too, unless its auto.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||04-21-2001||07:40 PM||||could be rear main or oil pan. Is there motor oil inside the bell housing? If so rear main or the plug in the end of the cam bore. (You can check that by pulling the shield off.) REar main also tends to leave tracks on the flywheel, but not always.<P>If it's only running down the sheild then it could be just the oil pan gasket. As far as I can remember..... The pan gasket can't get oil inside the bell housing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Elenor84||04-22-2001||12:39 PM||||What I noticed most is that the starter is drenched in oil...????????<BR>think thats from the flywheel or that side of the oil pan gasket?<BR>thx<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Indiana_resto_guy||04-22-2001||02:47 PM||||Did you go and hose the thing off?<BR>If your starter is wet with oil, how about above it?<BR>Is your valve cover wet in the front?<BR>A rear main won't leak that much, however a valve cover gasket will. As the engine runs oil pools in the top of the head for lubrication, if that gasket is bad oil will seep out in buckets!||||reg||1
Z||000014||862M4inCA||04-22-2001||11:52 PM||||hrm. a quart a day, fresh wet drippy oily starter, and all on a 2.5? If it's leaking a quart a day I would definitely think you would be able to see a good steady drip or stream of oil wherever it's coming from, so might check that, otherwise it sounds like the valve cover to me. They have a tendency when using the cork gasket to leak, in some cases quite profusely. Try replacing the valve cover with a rubber gasket. I know Autozone has them for about $7. Might also check to make sure all the bolts are tight, and should check and see if you have excess oil in the spark plug holes (oil will drip from the valve cover onto the head and pool into the plug recesses). If that's a no-go the next likely culprit would probably be the oil pan gasket. The lip of the oil pan rides just over the starter and if it's loose or leaking could be throwing oil onto the starter as well. Hope some of this leads you in the right direction.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Elenor84||04-23-2001||01:02 AM||||There's only one problem...I just replaced the valve cover gasket about 5 months ago...but u knwo what...I felt kinda weird wen I put it back on...as if I made a mistake or something...I was in a hurry...maybe I'll replace it again...and I'll do the oil pan gasket...<BR>but just for reference...were exactly is the rear main seal?  Is it wwerre the tranny meets the engine?<P>Anyways thx for all your help guys...<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||yellowTee||04-23-2001||03:49 AM||||Yes, the rear mainseal is just what it says, it is the last seal of the crankshaft. If that is leaking it will generaly leave a little puddle right under the car where the engine and tranny meet.||||reg||1
